ard and the shins drop down to meet the support beam.\n\nI'm calculating the two-segment leg reach against the target foot position, finding it's bent since the distance is shorter than full extension, so I'm setting the knee to bend upward and outward. I'm placing the foot paw at the beam's top surface and spacing five toes along the z-axis to wrap around the beam.\n\nRealizing the paws would overlap if placed too close to center, so I'm recalculating the angle to push them outward—settling on roughly 35° from the beam top, giving each paw center around x=0.05 with a slight lift off the surface.\n\nNow I'm positioning toe rings around each paw and hiding the first toe under the paw's starting edge, then setting paw proportions. For the legs, I'm thinking through the hip joint hierarchy with thigh sweeps, adding a rounded rump sphere behind the body, and considering that koalas don't have visible tails so I'll skip that feature.\n\nI'm writing the fur painting logic to add white speckled patches on the rump, using noise and randomness to scatter lighter pixels where the surface normal and height meet certain thresholds. Writing fur paint function...\n\nAdding darker claw tones, cream ear fluff ramps, and a light grey muzzle gradient; then sketching bark texture with noise, three eucalyptus leaf color variants, and small brown gum nut clusters for twig detail.\n\nI'm thinking about a small pixel-art plaque on the base front with a leaf icon and dots instead of text, keeping the stand itself simple. For the back of the koala, I'm adding dither speckles along the spine and a small rounded bum patch, while skipping shoulder pads and leaving chest fur as tinted texture cards.\n\nDesigning tuft texture pixels...\n\nNow I'm questioning whether cards sharing the same texture but different tints would each need their own atlas slot, which could get expensive if tints vary per card — I should check cards.ts to see how tinting is actually implemented before assuming.\n\n",
